# Security Standards (strong but practical)

## Trust boundaries
- Treat ALL external input as hostile: request bodies, query params, headers,
  cookies, file uploads, third-party API responses, webhook payloads.
- Validate on the server. Client-side validation is UX only, never security.

## Injection prevention
- Use parameterized queries / prepared statements / ORM bindings ONLY.
  NEVER build SQL, NoSQL, LDAP, or shell commands by string concatenation.
- Never pass user input into eval/exec/shell/templates without strict escaping.

## Output handling
- Escape/encode all dynamic data at the output point (HTML, JS, URL, CSS).
- Set a Content-Security-Policy; avoid inline scripts where possible.

## Authentication
- Hash passwords with bcrypt / argon2 / scrypt (salted, slow).
  NEVER store plaintext; never use md5/sha1 for passwords.
- Use secure, httpOnly, sameSite cookies or short-lived signed tokens with
  expiry and a refresh strategy.

## Authorization (the #1 real-world breach class)
- Check authorization on EVERY protected action, not just authentication.
- Verify the caller may access the SPECIFIC resource (object-level auth):
  a user requesting /orders/123 must be confirmed to own order 123.
- Default-deny: a missing permission check must fail closed.

## Secrets
- NEVER hardcode secrets, API keys, passwords, or tokens. Load from env / a
  secrets manager. Never log secrets, tokens, passwords, full PII, or full PANs.

## Transport & data
- Assume HTTPS/TLS everywhere; never disable certificate verification.
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest where supported.
- Least privilege for DB users, API scopes, and cloud roles.

## Common protections
- CSRF protection on state-changing requests using cookie sessions.
- Rate-limit auth, password-reset, and abuse-prone endpoints.
- Validate uploads (type, size); store outside web root; never trust the
  client-supplied filename or content-type.
- Set headers: HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options / CSP.

## Errors must not leak
- Generic error messages to clients; full detail logged server-side only.
- Never expose stack traces, SQL, file paths, or internal hostnames to users.

## Dependencies
- Prefer maintained libraries over hand-rolled crypto/auth/parsing.
- Flag any dependency added; avoid abandoned or known-vulnerable packages.

> Mark security-sensitive logic in code with a `SECURITY:` comment.
